AUDIO: 'stars. Hitting number one on GitHub trending at the end of June. Here\'s the thing most people get wrong. It is not a cloud service like'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'OpenRouter. There\'s no account, no markup, no company in the middle. It\'s a gateway that runs on your own machine. One process, one dashboard, and one OpenAI compatible endpoint on localhost.'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Behind that endpoint, 268 providers, over 500 models. Claude, GPT, Gemini, Cohere, DeepSeek, and more than 90'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'providers with free tiers. Their documentation counts roughly 1 and 1/2 billion documented free tokens per month across those tiers. They\'re approximation, and they say so. Your keys stay local, encrypted at rest.'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then the two signature tricks. One, routing. 18 strategies. But the headline is a model called auto. You ask for auto, and it picks the best'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'available route. And when a provider dies mid-session, it cascades. Subscription first, then API keys, then cheap providers, then free ones. They claim an 8-ms average average'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'failover, their dashboard number. Not an independent benchmark, so hold it loosely. Two, compression. Tool outputs, get diffs, logs, test dumps,'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get squeezed before they write to the provider. 15 to 95% token savings, they claim. Averaging around 89% on tool-heavy sessions. Again, their numbers.'

[06:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, the mechanism is open-source. You can read every line of it. That\'s the signal tower. Now, let\'s actually build the junction. Setup. Two installs, and I\'ll flag every trap I know about.'

[06:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Install one, the locomotive. One line in your terminal. Curl the install script from open-code.ai, pipe it to bash. Or, use npm or homebrew if you prefer.'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Done. Install two, the signal tower. npm install {dash}g omniroot. Then type one word, omniroot, and it starts.'

[06:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Trap number one. It wants a recent node, version 22 or 24 on a current patch. If it refuses to start, update node first. Their own troubleshooting doc says'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s the most common failure. Now, open localhost, port 20128, and you\'re standing inside the dashboard. Providers on the left, routing in the middle, usage on the right.'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Connect your first providers. A few free ones need no sign-up at all. For the rest, you click connect, sign in to the provider, and the key lands in your local vault. Connect your paid accounts, too, if you'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have them. Remember, the keys stay on your machine. Last step here. Go to the endpoints page, and copy your omniroot API key. On a purely local setup, it\'s a'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'placeholder. Literally SK_OmniRoot. Both machines are now idling. Time to couple them. The coupling is one command.'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is the part almost nobody covering these tools shows you. In your terminal, OmniRoot config open code --base URL localhost:20128'

[08:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '--api key your key. That\'s it. It writes OpenCode\'s config file for you. And it merges politely. So, any providers you already had stay untouched. Look inside the file. It\'s at'

[08:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '.config/opencod/opencod.json. And you\'ll see exactly what happened. A new provider called OmniRoot pointing at your localhost endpoint /v1'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a starter catalog of models. Now, my one strong recommendation, add one more model to that list named simply auto.'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That single word exposes OmniRoot\'s whole routing brain inside OpenCode. You stop choosing models and start choosing outcomes. Launch OpenCode, open the model picker, and there it is.'

[08:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'OmniRoot sitting next to your other providers with auto at the top. Two traps before you write. On Windows, there\'s a known open issue number 3330 where the generator writes the config to the wrong folder.'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If the provider doesn\'t appear, check that the file landed in your .config directory. And if you set this up long ago and get 404 errors, old configs sometimes doubled the /v1 in'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the URL. Just rerun the generator. It regenerates clean. That\'s the whole build. One agent, one junction, every model behind one picker. So, what does it actually feel like when'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the wall comes? You\'re deep in a session riding a free tier and the provider throws a quota error. Here\'s the sequence under the hood. The circuit breaker trips on that'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provider. The router consults the fallback ladder. Subscription accounts first, then API keys, then cheap providers, then the free pool, and resends your request down the next'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open track. Your agent never sees the failure. The next token just arrives from somewhere else. And the compression story compounds it. The biggest token hog in agent coding'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isn\'t your prompts. It\'s tool output. Every git diff, every log dump, every test run writes the wire on every single request. Omni route squeezes exactly that before it leaves your machine and there\'s a'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guard that discards the compression whenever it would somehow make the prompt bigger. Squeeze the freight and the same free tier hauls five, 10, sometimes 20 times more work. One honest caution before you trust any'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the numbers on the dashboard. The 8 milliseconds, the 99% success rate, the 89% savings all self-reported by the project. Independent benchmarks don\'t exist yet. The project is 5 months old.'

